{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nParity sibling of the reconverge merge-abort recoveries (loris/1757 variant (a), wolverine/1767 variant (b)): the push non-fast-forward recovery path has the SAME untracked-overwrite wedge. `rebar._store.push_recovery._recover_dirty_merge` sets a dirty tree aside with a stash COMMIT OBJECT (`_stash_create`, never refs/stash), clears TRACKED changes via `git reset --hard` (untracked files are deliberately left alone, matching `stash push` semantics), then merges the remote ref. When an UNTRACKED local file collides with a file the merge wants to create — exactly the compaction-leftover class (`*-SNAPSHOT.json`, `*.retired`) that wedged reconverge — the merge aborts (\"untracked working tree files would be overwritten by merge\"), and today's code gives up: `merge --abort`, restore stash, warn/strict-raise. Every push retry then re-fails the same way: the push path cannot self-heal the store class we already heal in reconverge.\n\n## Approach\nExtend `_recover_dirty_merge` (and its `_merge_remote_under_lock` sibling seam if the abort surfaces there) with the SAME recovery shape sync.py's reconverge uses: on merge failure, parse the paths git names with the parser in `rebar._store.sync` — today `_untracked_overwrite_paths` (sync.py:226), being generalized by prerequisite Gerrit change 1767 (ticket dozing-trophied-wolverine) into a shared `_merge_abort_named_paths`; use whichever name is on main at implementation time — move the named UNTRACKED colliding paths into the shared quarantine directory (`<git-common-dir>/reconverge-quarantine/<utc-ts>/`, the dir `_quarantine_untracked`/sync.py:248 established — move, never delete), retry the merge ONCE; any other failure keeps today's behavior exactly (abort, restore stash, warn or strict-raise). Reuse boundary, respecting each module's git seam: the pure PARSER is imported from sync (no subprocess in it), but the MOVER is not — sync's `_quarantine_untracked` shells through sync's module-level `_git`, whereas every push_recovery function deliberately late-binds `core._git` so push's ~25-site test monkeypatch keeps working (push_recovery.py:10-27). So implement the quarantine move locally in push_recovery through `core._git`/os.rename against the same quarantine directory path (share the dir-path helper if 1767 exposes one), rather than calling sync's mover across the seam. The stash restore ordering is preserved: quarantine + retry happen between the failed merge and any `_restore_stash`, so the stash commit object is restored exactly once on every exit path.\n\nSequencing: depends on change 1767 (wolverine) being merged — it lands the shared parser/quarantine helpers this reuses.\n\n## Scope\n- src/rebar/_store/push_recovery.py — untracked-overwrite recovery (quarantine + single retry) in the dirty-merge and locked-merge recovery paths; reuse sync's shared helpers.\n- tests/unit/test_push_recovery_untracked.py (new; or extend the existing push_recovery test module if one covers `_recover_dirty_merge`) — RED-first coverage.\n\n## Testing\n- RED first: fixture store where the worktree holds an untracked file that the remote ref's merge wants to create; assert today's `_recover_dirty_merge` fails (merge aborted, stash restored, no merge commit).\n- GREEN: same fixture after the fix — the colliding untracked file is moved into the quarantine dir (bytes preserved, byte-for-byte), the retried merge completes with both parents, and the stash-recorded tracked edits are restored.\n- Guard: a genuine content conflict still aborts, restores the stash, and warns/strict-raises (unchanged behavior); paths git does not name are untouched; the quarantine dir is only created when something is moved.\n- Stash-safety: the stash commit object is restored exactly once on both the retry-success and retry-failure paths (no lost tracked edits).\n- Mutation check: skipping the quarantine move or the retry returns the RED assertion; retrying more than once is killed by a call-count assertion.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] A push recovery whose merge aborts ONLY on untracked-overwrite collisions completes after quarantining the named paths and retrying once, with no event bytes lost (quarantine holds the moved files); checked by the RED-to-GREEN test.\n- [ ] Tracked edits set aside in the stash are restored on every exit path (success, retry-failure, non-recoverable abort); checked by the stash-safety tests.\n- [ ] Genuine conflicts and unnamed paths keep today's behavior exactly; checked by the guard test.\n- [ ] The recovery reuses the shared sync parser/quarantine helpers (no duplicated recovery mechanism); checked by review of the diff.\n- [ ] `make lint` and `make typecheck` clean; lands via Gerrit (LLM-Review +1 AND Verified +1) before close."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786690172674617001,"uuid":"a0c07d36-25d5-4b83-9133-c2f91cb9ddb0"}